What I suggest the planner picks
1. #1202, step 1 (filed by @yuanhao today). Have the bash safety checks read a parsed command tree instead of raw text. Extract every simple command, including those inside $(…), subshells, backticks and strings, and run the existing checks on each one as well as on the raw text. Anything that fails to parse asks for confirmation instead of being auto-approved. The tests should include #1196's shape and the two nested-command shapes from #1193 that are still open.
2. #1203. I confirmed by reading the source that /side and /architect never set max_tokens on any provider, not just Bedrock, so they ignore the user's setting everywhere. The editor agent already passes it, which shows the fix. I haven't run this.

Research
• Claude Code and Codex both parse with tree-sitter. When they can't get a reliable command line out of the tree, they ask the user instead of auto-approving. Claude Code returns "ask" for any command substitution.
• opencode strips leading VAR=... assignments before matching its rules, except when the assignment contains $(…). That's a good near-miss test for #1202.
• Two parsers to choose between for #1202:
  • brush-parser is pure Rust and can break down text inside double quotes, which is exactly the #1196 case.
  • tree-sitter-bash is what the other tools use, but it needs a C build.
• The quickest way to choose is to run both over the existing safety test commands and count how many fail to parse.
• I saved these findings to yopedia. Searching it beforehand found nothing earlier on this topic.
